---
description: Architecture rules for LLM calls, jobs, persistence, and cost control
alwaysApply: true
---

1. NEVER one LLM call per persona. Generate personas in batches (25-50 per call) and simulate in batches (20-30 per call). For very large audiences, simulate a stratified sample of "anchor" personas in full detail and extrapolate segment weights to the rest, clearly labeled as extrapolated.
2. Every LLM call returns JSON validated by Zod, with retry + repair on failure. All LLM access goes through lib/llm.ts (retry, JSON repair, token logging).
3. Prompt templates live in /prompts as versioned files.
4. Long work runs as background jobs with status: queued → generating → simulating → analyzing → complete/failed. UI shows live progress. Jobs are resumable, cancellable, and tolerate partial failure.
5. Persist everything: ideas, audiences, personas, reactions, experiments, results.
6. Seeded random demographics so reruns are comparable. Same seed + same audience = comparable A/B results.
7. Cost guard: show estimated cost before running, per-user limits, hard cap per experiment.
8. Every number shown in the UI must be computed in code from stored reactions. The LLM only writes narrative around computed stats.
9. Simulation prompts must fight positivity bias: personas stay in character, are realistically skeptical, and many should ignore or reject the product.
